Let us digress briefly to this passage from the Bible, which correctly reads: " Because you are lukewarm, neither cold nor hot, I will spit you out of my mouth." It speaks of "warm," which should be easy to understand for any serious seeker; because we associate everything that is flooded with light, caring and loving with warm, that is, that which directly corresponds to light. But "cold" is also mentioned in contrast to lukewarm, and that should be a mystery to many serious people, because we associate spiritual coldness with egoism, isolation, indifference to the well-being of our fellow human beings and with lovelessness. But this mystery seems to me to be easily solved if we take the law of reciprocity into account. According to this, everything we put out in thoughts, words and deeds must necessarily come back to us - for better or for worse. However, if the reaction of an icy and unkind attitude falls on the person concerned, he will have to suffer more from these things, which at some point he will no longer be able to bear and, through painful experiences, will usually cause him to rethink his attitudes and take a better path. He therefore has significantly better prospects of advancement than a lukewarm, intellectually indifferent person, since the latter has much weaker interactions that do not necessarily force him to confront his views. -

The image of the "sheep and goats" is well chosen for that time, even if they may initially be repulsive to modern people. Sheep symbolize obedience, goats the unruly. From a spiritual point of view, however, obedience is "going along with the laws of creation", while the unruly is a reluctance, a "resistance against the order of creation". So obedience is not to be equated with the loss of individuality, but rather it is simply about a beneficial, constructive, helpful and supportive action when fitting into the natural order - which on the contrary brings a distinct, harmonious individuality instead of wiping it out. In contrast, resisting the constructive forces of creation means destructive, destroying, exploitative and selfish action.


